  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 956 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nWe have a lull in storm activity this evening. Looking at\nthe surface analysis, the cold front has stalled south of the \nKansas state line as high pressure has spread across eastern \nNebraska. Over the next few hours we'll see the nocturnal low- \nlevel jet ramp up triggering more showers and storms north of \nthe surface front in a region with moderate elevated instability\nand 40-50 kt of shear through the cloud layer. This could lead \nto some more organized storm development capable of producing \nquarter-size hail and strong to damaging winds in addition to \nlocally heavy rainfall. While the severe threat still seems \nfairly marginal, it will greatly depend on the amount of \nelevated instability can be generated as the LLJ advects warmer \nair just above the frontal boundary while the Canadian High \npulls cooler air south. \n\nExpect storms to start to redevelop around 11pm along a \ncorridor near and just south of I-80, rapidly expanding in \ncoverage and tracking east into Iowa overnight. Storm motions \nparallel to the front would support some training of storms \novernight which could lead to localized flash flooding, \nespecially over our southwest Iowa counties into Wednesday \nmorning. Once the first round clears around 7am, CAMs show a \npotential second wave that will move in shortly after, bringing \na more scattered chance for mostly showers at this point. The \nnocturnal low-level jet should be waning, decreasing any \nelevated instability we have left that isn't chewed through by \nthe overnight storms. Rain chances should taper off through the \nafternoon.\n\nTaking a step back and looking at the upper-level pattern, we're\nseeing a trough in the jet stream pull this Canadian air mass\nsouth into the Central Plains, and these storms are developing\non the southern edge of this air mass. Wednesday night into\nThursday we'll start to see this Canadian surface high slide\neastward, bringing back southerly low-level flow while the ridge\nover the Desert Southwest starts to expand eastward. This will\nstart our warming trend going into the weekend. Temperatures\nwill still be very pleasant on Thursday with highs in the low-\nto-mid 80s. Temperatures will get warmer each day going into the\nweekend with southerly flow only strengthening ahead of a\ndeveloping area of low pressure on the lee-side of the Rockies.\nThis will amplify the low-level jet Friday into Saturday\nhelping push temperatures back into the upper 80s to low 90s for\nhighs over the weekend. \n\nWith a strong nocturnal low-level jet in place as well as active\nand diffluent northwesterly flow at the jet level, we could see\na chance for nocturnal storms return Saturday night into Sunday,\nand again with a more substantial shortwave Sunday night into\nMonday. The pattern going into next week looks to hold fairly\nsteady, characterized by a ridge over the Desert Southwest and \na broad trough over the Ohio River Valley. This should keep us \nin the transition zone under northwesterly flow aloft, giving us\nseasonably warm temperatures and more chances for showers and \nstorms through the week. This is reflected in the CPC's 6-10 day\noutlook by a forecast leaning warmer and wetter.",
